./xmodel/groktransport.out:1:I'll start by reading the review skill and the transport documents so the attack can be grounded in what was actually claimed and checked.This is a mathematical audit, not a git-diff review. I’ll load G2 from REDUCTION.md, run the checker, and replay the lemma against what the machine actually verifies.Checker passed. Next I’ll read the G2/T-chain definitions and the fixtures so the scope claim can be checked against what was actually proved.The lemma looks formally tight; I’m now checking Sigray’s exact NF conditions, whether residue-A/td-7 numbers are live, and what G2 actually required.I’m checking GGV’s NE-corner claim, the live `8_28` record, Sigray Lemma 2.1, and whether the residue-A poles are the ones the checker hardcodes.Replay of 1.1–2.1 found no break. Next I’ll write the review: VERDICT first, findings ranked, and only `xmodel/grok-transport-review.md` will be added.**VERDICT: SOUND-WITH-ERRATA.** Review is at [`xmodel/grok-transport-review.md`](xmodel/grok-transport-review.md). Checker: exit 0, `"status": "PASS"`. No other repo files were touched.
./xmodel/groktransport.out:3:Theorems 1.1 and 2.1 replay, and I did not find a break in the cusp-peak argument. The document proves a real T2→T4 simultaneous-normalization theorem for the selected pre-Laurent GGV pair; that is not G1 restated. It does not repair G2 as REDUCTION CRITICAL 3 defines it (no corner-to-tree map). The machine gate does not verify any of the theorems.
./xmodel/groktransport.out:9:2. **High — scope.** This is T2 ⇒ enhanced T4: the selected pair is already lex-minimal, so \(C\) and \(R\) suffice and the GGV ledger is recoverable. That closes the T4 *normalization* fork. It is not a restatement of G1, and it is not CRITICAL 3 (GGV chain ↦ Sigray tree). T5–T10 stay unchanged. Conjecture T is correctly open.
./xmodel/groktransport.out:11:3. **Clear — residue-A / td-7.** Correct refusal to identify them with `(8,28)` (degree pairs \(168\neq 72\), \(271320\neq 72\)). Corners and TDU masses match the cited sources. They are schema controls, not instance transports. Poles are hardcoded; td-7 uses `D_f` on one side and `d_g` on the other.
./xmodel/groktransport.out:13:4. **Residual — attack (1) fails.** The LND inequality, unique-northeast maximizer, and lex bound close. Weighted Jung–van der Kulk is not a hidden dependency.
./xmodel/groktransport.out:15:5. **Erratum — three false sentences, none load-bearing.** Corollary 1.2’s “the scalar”; “stronger than global GGV minimality”; “precisely” Sigray (iii) for a strict \(l_f<k_f\).
