xmodel/sol-jvdk.log:2371:./dist/jc72108-theory-bundle-v1/xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2815:| 2 | **Hurwitz factorization of \(\hat g:\overline{C}\to\mathbb{P}^1\) in \(S_6\)** | Classical battery used RH as a *number*. Existence of an actual degree-6 cover with the pinned local types is a different obstruction; failure kills residue-A independently of Sigray. | From Newton pairs \((7,2)(3,10)(2,5)\) at each pole, compute the local monodromy cycle type on the 6 sheets of \(\hat g\) (poles of order 3). Check whether two such classes, plus the \(x\)-side cluster (\(g\) finite there) and RH-constrained finite ramification, multiply to \(1\) in \(S_6\). |
xmodel/sol-jvdk.log:2374:./dist/jc72108-theory-bundle-v1/xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2824:Dim-2 keeps proving the statements that are true (GMC(2), Shaska for every weight sign, no graded plane CE). Every known \(n\ge 3\) mechanism dies in the plane (Picard/\(d=3\) needle, no tangent-sweep, no graded escape). Eighty-five years without an example is evidence. Residue-A is the best formal candidate I have seen in this literature, but “passes the printed list” is exactly Theorem O, and an étale non-proper degree-6 endomorphism of \(\mathbb{A}^2\) is the thing JC2 says does not exist — consistency at vertices is cheap compared with algebraizing 126 series.
xmodel/sol-jvdk.log:2387:./dist/jc72108-theory-bundle-v1/xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:3041:MONDELLO-CHECK.md:37:> **Remark.** The characteristic restrictions in our results are not an artifact of the method. Mondello's plane Keller pair P = x + x²y + x⁴ + x⁶y², Q = y + x⁵ + x⁶y + x⁷y² + x⁸y³ over F₂ (arXiv:2608.02634) has Jacobian determinant 1, sends the three distinct points (0,1), (1,0), (1,1) to a common image, and generates a degree 3 separable field extension, so even the separable Jacobian conjecture fails in characteristic two. Its Newton polygons form a strip pair along the direction (1,1) satisfying our vertex normalization, and in any characteristic other than two our near-origin eliminations, whose pivot constants at this polygon data are the lattice determinants 2, 4, and 6, force the strip corners to vanish and reduce any such pair to an elementary automorphism. In characteristic two every one of these determinants vanishes, the corresponding bracket equations become identically zero, and the strips survive; this is precisely the escape that Mondello's example exploits.
xmodel/sol-jvdk.log:2388:./dist/jc72108-theory-bundle-v1/xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:3046:SHEET6.md:10:- **1c. RECON correction**: "no independent reproof of ≤5 exists" is WRONG. Sigray's thesis Theorem 9.1 proves td(f,g) ≥ 6 for any counterexample, independently of Żołądek, same year, different machinery (decorated Eggers–Wall trees vs Newton–Puiseux charts). Never journal-published (zbMATH lists only his monodromy + kp′q−lpq′ papers) but cited as the bound by Makar-Limanov (arXiv:2106.06869) and Borisov (arXiv:1901.04073: "current best lower bound ... is 6"). Caveats on BOTH proofs: Żołądek's neighboring gcd sections have a **documented gap** (GGV arXiv:1401.1784: Lemma 4.10 "claims without proof that I₂ ⊂ (1/m)Γ(f₂)"; his claimed B>33 fails, B≥16 stands only via GGV's independent reproof) — his topdeg-5 sections have no reported gap but are unaudited; Sigray's thesis is unrefereed. Also flagged: arXiv:2407.13795 (Moskowicz, 2024, 8pp) claims NO Keller map has prime field-extension degree — unpublished, treat as unvetted; if ever verified it would exclude 7 and make 6, 8, 9 the whole frontier.
xmodel/sol-jvdk.log:2389:./dist/jc72108-theory-bundle-v1/xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:3047:SHEET6.md:14:- **(A) Żołądek's Newton–Puiseux charts** (reconstructed from zbMATH + Nguyen Van Chau arXiv:0804.3172v2 + MIMUW seminar abstract): resolve (f,g) at infinity in BOTH source and target; near each divisor use multivalued charts (x^{−1/m}, ξ) — truncated fractional-power series x(Σc_k x^{−k/m} + ξx^{−n/m}) where ξ replaces the Puiseux tail ("left and right Newton-Puiseux charts", Chau). The Keller map becomes near-monomial in charts; jac≡1 forces rigid exponent+leading-coefficient relations. Topological degree = sheets over infinity = Σ local degrees over dicritical divisors, with Orevkov's identity Σ(ramification excess at infinity) = td − 1. Case analysis = finite enumeration of dicritical/chart configurations for td ≤ 5; each killed by exponent arithmetic or analytic contradiction.
